// KAN KAI-9 — test scene: address-in-the-language + inline channel override.
//
// Both actors declare their OUTPUT ADDRESS in BPScript (transport.midi(ch:N)) — the
// host invents no routing. `lead` plays on MIDI channel 1, `bass` on channel 2.
// The note `E4(ch:5)` carries an INLINE CHANNEL OVERRIDE (canon form `(ch:N)` on a
// nude note, bpscript msg [293]): that single note jumps to channel 5, the rest of
// `lead` stays on its declared channel 1.
//
// Purpose: exercise the KAI-9 big-bang — once Kairos materializes the output layer
// from the tree (and the host drops pickTransport / the actor→transport map), this
// scene proves the per-actor address AND the per-note override travel in the tree,
// not via any host-side routing map.

@core
@tempo:120

@actor lead  alphabet.western  transport.midi(ch:1)
@actor bass  alphabet.western  transport.midi(ch:2)

S -> {Lead, Low}

Lead -> lead.C4 E4(ch:5) lead.G4 lead.C4
Low  -> bass.C2 bass.G2 bass.C2 bass.E2
